arate  into two separate groups  when we separated them by sham versus  sepsis surgery and we combined all of  the day six and day zero samples  and the alzheimer's and the and the wild  type samples  we can see two different groups and  there's a cluster of six samples  that are highlighted in yellow  next we looked at the distances between  the  the day zero samples represented by the  small circles  and the day six samples represented by  the large circles  and so the and so the longer the the  distance  represents more um more of a change  within a given um animal between day six  and day zero and again you can see that  the longer distances  are are are in that cluster of six  samples  next we also looked at the treatments um  at the treatment groups and what you um  and separated them  into wild-type sham and sepsis and ad  shaman sepsis and you'll see that this  cluster  of six of six samples  which we refer to as a day six  uh sepsis cluster again is comprised of  sepsis samples  and an equal number are from both our  wild type  and our alzheimer's disease sepsis group  this is what we focused on for the  remainder  of our of our analyses and we asked the  question  what are the factors that drove those  six samples to cluster together  the first question we asked were any  taxa decreased  at day six compared to day zero  so first i will orient you  to our graph plot and what we've done  here  is that instead of separating out the  groups  by their treatment we've actually  clustered those six samples together  as shown um by the red box  and what you can see is that if you  focus on the mirror baculum  um genera  which are all shown in uh what you're  all shown  in green is that the day six sample  uh day six cluster samples have almost  less than five percent mirror baculum  while um all the other samples at either  day zero or day six  have a greater than fifty percent  percentage  of mere baculum species  then we asked whether any taxa were  were increased at day six compared to  day zero  here it was easier to see changes by  by looking at the family level and  that's why the colors are different here  the orange orangish yellow color  uh represents your uh  mirror baculaceae species and what  you'll see  is that in that day6 sepsis sepsis  cluster group  that you have an increased number of um  of uh of a taxa from the claustro dallas  family i mean order and the burke  holdery and the bulk burke  holdery holder eat alleys order as well  and these and these are represented by  three  uh three primary families uh the  pepto streptococc cockadacei  and the claustrodacia families and the  burkhold burkhold arealis uh families as  well  then we asked the question were there  any taxa that had a protective effect  against the clostridale's colonization  at day six so again here  i've switched back to separating our  samples  by the treatment groups and this makes  it easier to see  these changes and the samples  that are in the day six cluster here are  outlined in the red  in the greenish blue boxes what we can  see  is at the genus level that the samples  that have a that have a greater loss  of a mirror baculum as shown in  as shown in green also have a  low level of lactobacillus  at day zero so you can see that by  comparing  the the panel on the left and the panel  on the right  for all of the samples in the boxes and  these lactobacillus species are  represented by  uh lactobacillus murinous rutary  and john sonny these comprise  nearly 98 of all the lactobacillus  samples in our mice what we also found  was that samples with a low  lactobacillus at day zero  are also colonized by several of those  clostridiales families that i showed you  on the previous slide again we're  looking at the genus level  and the lactobacillus samples are shown  in  blue and in the highlighted boxes  you can see when comparing dasier on the  left and day six on the right  that that you have an increase  of both the purple and the o