bers  of the cost of the clostridiales order  postsepsis but importantly we also found  that there was  that there were no difference in um in  the abundance  of of an ataxa between our wild type and  our transgenic mice  sepsis so this leads us to the  conclusion  um that potentially since there appears  to be a protective effect  of lactobacillus species um  that that we could look at um at  the administration of lactobacillus  as a as a strains or species as a  as a preventative or a protective  approach  to treat um gut dysbiosis  that's associated with sepsis this has  been tried  um in many uh earlier clinical trials  but we identified some specific  lactobacillus  strains that have not been employed in  some of the earlier clinical trials  so uh so we'd like to look at these at  these further  namely lactobacillus rootary  um and some john sony uh strains that we  identified  in our study so  um i'd like to wrap up with  acknowledging all of the people who  contributed  to this work alison burchasek  is a graduate student in my lab who's  very talented  and spearheaded most of this work  we were also we were also assisted  by shoreline microbiome who performed  the  sequencing and introduced us introduced  us to the strain id kit  and sequencing was also performed um at  the  at the pac at the pac bio core facility  at the university  of delaware and i'd also like to thank  our funding sources from the nih as well  as um  lab roots for inviting me to speak  as part of of the symposium and with  that i'd like to say  thank you for watching and please feel  free to  to contact me if you have any questions  thank you
 

